# PBPS boundary56: independent primary-first source assessment

Pinned primary: Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Start via the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler, arXiv:2609.06905v1, supplied immutable HTML. No candidate Lean, API, proof body, prior review verdict, or whole_math directory was inspected.

The original assumptions are V in C2(R^d), 0 < alpha <= beta, alpha I <= Hessian V(x) <= beta I for every x, and eta in (0,1/beta]. Lemma B.1 assumes beta eta <= 1. No higher-derivative or compact-support assumption on V appears. Part (1) quantifies over every f in L2(pi_eta^Y), without a mean-zero restriction.

Source objects stay fixed: target mu(dx) proportional to exp(-V(x)) dx; joint J = pi_eta from (2.6); nu = pi_eta^Y; X~mu and independent Z~N(0,I); Y+=X+sqrt(eta)Z, Y-=X-sqrt(eta)Z. In source-based notation only, R=U_PP with Rf(y)=E[f(Y-)|Y+=y], and S=U_perpP with representative Sf(x,y)=f(2x-y)-Rf(y). This is a source dictionary, not a certificate about any unopened candidate definition. Source mu is the X-target, not the Y-marginal.

Appendix C.1 first restricts the gradient calculation to f in C_c^infinity(R^d). The conditional density p_y(u) is proportional to exp(-V((y+u)/2)-||y-u||^2/(8 eta)); the parameter score is s_y(u)=-1/2 grad V((y+u)/2)-(y-u)/(4 eta). Equation C.1 gives grad Rf(y)=Cov(f(Y-),s_y(Y-)|Y+=y). The conditional potential Hessian is at least (alpha+eta^-1)/4 times I; ||grad_u s_y||op <= (eta^-1-alpha)/4. Conditional Poincare plus Cauchy-Schwarz gives ||grad Rf(y)||^2 <= (eta^-1-alpha)^2/[4(alpha+eta^-1)] Var(f(Y-)|Y+=y). The integrated sharper estimate C.2 is eta ||grad Rf||^2 <= [(1-alpha eta)^2/(4(1+alpha eta))] ||Sf||^2. In particular 4 eta ||grad Rf||^2 <= ||Sf||^2.

The explicit normalized p_y law is available for every y under the standing source assumptions. This does not license upgrading the rough L2 representative to an every-y differentiation theorem: B.1 is an L2 quotient/domain statement, D.1 treats equality almost everywhere, and C.8 explicitly has nu-almost every y. Rough conditional expectation, gradient, and fiber square-integrability must keep their almost-everywhere scopes unless a source-backed producer establishes a stronger representative. The smooth compact-core calculation and the all-L2 passage are different edges.

Compact support of f does not give compact support of Rf. For a nonnegative nonzero smooth compact bump, p_y is strictly positive, so its conditional mean is positive for every y and has noncompact support. Therefore an extension using closedness first needs to put each Rf_n in the SAME weighted gradient domain with gradient given by C.1 and controlled by C.2. An operator graph containing only C_c^infinity input-output pairs does not admit Rf_n merely from compact support of f_n. This is a prerequisite to prove, not a public source hypothesis.

The primary invokes density and closedness of the gradient at the start of C.1 and H1 density in the proof of C.8; it does not provide a formal definition of weighted H1 or a proof identifying it with an authored graph closure. D.1 defines only L2 explicitly. The faithful convention is weighted Sobolev/weak gradient on nu, not an arbitrary closed relation labeled H1. A local graph-closure definition needs its relation to that genuine weighted gradient, compact-core density, uniqueness/closedness, and noncompact conditional-mean admission. These are proof ingredients to discharge internally. The omitted analytic bridge is not evidence that B.13 is false and does not authorize stronger public assumptions.

A source-only extension route is: approximate arbitrary f in L2(nu) by smooth compact f_n; use the same R contraction to obtain Rf_n -> Rf; apply the compact estimate to differences to make grad Rf_n Cauchy in L2(nu;R^d); use the already admitted same-gradient-domain pairs and closedness to obtain Rf in H1 with that limit gradient; pass the estimate to the limit. This route must not assume its endpoint domain membership. It has not been proved in Lean in this preread.

B.9 identifies ||Sf||^2=E Var(f(Y-)|Y+) in the original same joint law. B.5 and B.10/B.11 additionally identify ||Sf||^2=||f||^2-||Rf||^2=||Gamma_P f||^2, where Gamma_P=(I-R^2)^(1/2) is the nonnegative operator square root. A variance/residual identity or all-L2 gradient bound alone does not construct or certify Gamma, its square-root semantics, or the B.14/B.15 spectral coercivity. B.13 part (1) is all-L2; the rho_mac spectral bound and gamma_gap lower bound are on the mean-zero macroscopic subspace.

Appendix C.2 (the subsection) has a different half-turn object: q_y is the normalized conditional law of (X-y)/sqrt(eta), g_y=sqrt(eta) grad V(y+sqrt(eta)w), Sigma_y=eta^-1 Cov(X|Y=y). Under beta eta <= 1/2, C.11 is uniform in y and a: ||(I+H_y) ell_a,y||^2 <= C beta eta a^T(I-Sigma_y)a, with universal C. For measurable a_y with finite weighted quadratic integral, the joint centered affine function belongs to microscopic L2 and its integrated estimate holds. This half-turn estimate is different from the compact-core gradient estimate (equation C.2). Its C.12/C.13 prerequisites and later global B.17 remain outside boundary56.

Source proof graph: standing model -> B.8/B.9 same-law reflection/variance -> conditional normalized density and score -> curvature/score-derivative bounds -> conditional Poincare -> conditional Cauchy-Schwarz -> compact C.2 -> noncompact Rf_n weighted-gradient-domain admission -> L2 compact density + R contractivity + gradient difference control + gradient closedness -> all-L2 weighted-domain estimate. Orthogonal reflection block identities + genuine positive square root -> Gamma equalities are separate dependencies. Each omitted analytic bridge remains visible until independently verified.

Outcome: PRIMARY_SOURCE_CONTRACT_SEALED_ONLY. Candidate fidelity, Lean compilation, domain identification, Gamma construction, and formal admission are NOT_ASSESSED. Compiler was not started. No canonical Lean, audit, registry, ledger, site, or shared aggregation was changed.
